flvideo -- the FreeLFS video player ("Videos")
==============================================

    flvideo [--start SECONDS] [--pause] [FILE]

libmpv (recipe mpv, built without any window system or GPU code) demuxes, decodes, plays
the sound (PipeWire, else ALSA) and draws the subtitles with libass. Pictures come out of
mpv's software render API ("sw", vo=libmpv) into a buffer of this program, which flui puts
in the window under the control bar. That is slower than GPU rendering on a 4K file but
works on every machine, with no GL or EGL, and the memory use is the decoder plus one
window-sized frame (the render buffer is the size of the video area, so a small window is
also cheaper to scale into).

Hardware decoding: off by default since round 18 (software decoding; a Ryzen laptop did not play
anything, and VA-API through radeonsi/iHD is a path no QEMU test covers). FLVIDEO_HWDEC=auto-copy-safe
(or vaapi-copy) turns on VA-API decoding with the frames copied back to memory (mpv and the recipe
ffmpeg are built with libva). mpv's own log: ~/.cache/flvideo/mpv.log.

Controls: play/pause, -10 s and +10 s, a seek bar (drag or click), speed (click cycles
0.5x .. 2x), Audio and Subtitles menus (pick a track; Subtitles has "Off"), mute and volume,
Fullscreen. In fullscreen the bar hides after 3 s without mouse movement.
Keys: Space or K pause, Left/Right 5 s (Shift 30 s, Ctrl 60 s), J/L 10 s, Up/Down volume,
M mute, F or F11 fullscreen, Escape leaves fullscreen, V next subtitle track (then off),
A next audio track, [ and ] slower and faster, Q quit. Click the picture to pause, double
click for fullscreen.
Subtitles: tracks inside the file, and .srt/.ass/.sub files next to it with a similar name
(sub-auto=fuzzy).
Resume: the position is saved every 15 s and on exit to ~/.local/share/flvideo/resume.tsv and
used the next time the same file is opened (not within the first or the last 10 s; a file
that played to the end starts from the top).

Fullscreen opens a second window and closes the first (libflui has no fullscreen toggle on a
live window); playback continues in the same mpv instance.

Open: the Open button or Ctrl+O asks the desktop's file dialog (xdg-desktop-portal FileChooser, i.e.
flportal) for one file, filtered to videos with "All files" as the second filter. The dialog runs in
a child process (`flvideo --pick-file`, libdbus) so the window never waits; the chosen file replaces
the current one (its position is saved first, a remembered position of the new file is used). Without
the portal the window says so. libflui has no file dialog of its own.

Not done: playlists, DVD or network streams, chapters in the UI, subtitle delay, screenshots.

Test: make check needs the ffmpeg tool: it generates a clip (tests/mkclip.sh), renders a
frame at a seek position, and checks that a second run resumes at the same frame.
